Scitovsky
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So, From Scitovsky's 'External Economies'.
 He uses Meade's formulation; Meade talks about bees and orchards and blossom.
 This takes us to the basic point of `direct interdependence among producers'
 (p.144):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
x_{1}=F(l_{1},c_{1},...;x_{2},l_{2,}c_{2},...)\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Which doesn't really help us understand those inter-relations but at least
 says it's a function of the other firm's output.
 Scitovsky reckons this - which is from Meade - is a 'rigorous definition'.
 Hmm.
 So it's a 'technological external economy' - the other firm can actually
 have an impact on the production relations within the other firm.
 The relevant passage from Brakman et al p.38: ``With pure (or technological)
 external economies an increase in industry-wide output alters the technological
 relationship between inputs and output for each individual firm.
 It therefore has an impact on the firm's production function.'' Which is,
 of couse, what we see in the definition above.
 It's probably worth pointing out that the above definition is different
 from firm one having paid for inputs from firm 2 - which would be keeping
 things within the price system.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I wonder how I'd describe backyard's interdependence? Anyhoo - as he points
 out, 
\begin_inset Formula $F()$
\end_inset

 is a production function, and it contains elements from firm 2 in it.
 But note that when we get to Brakman, the emphasis is on how 
\series bold
`industry-wide' 
\series default
output affects that production function.
 I'd want to see the above definition defined to reflect that; I wonder
 how GE would want to describe that inter-relation? The above is more like
 how one might conceive of it in ABM, actually.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A quote: ``The examples of external economies given by Meade are somewhat
 bucolic in nature, having to do with bees, orchards, and woods.
 This, however, is no accident: it is not easy to find examples from industry.
 Going through the many examples of external economies quoted in the literature,
 I found only two that fit the above definition: the case in which a firm
 benefits from the labor market created by the establishment of other firms
 and that in which several firms use a resource which is free but limited
 in supply.'' (p.145) Examples of the latter he gives are all commons-related:
 production changes from oil-well drilling of others; fisherman in the same
 waters; congestion problems on a public road.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It's interesting that Scitovsky are saying they're so rare.
 For him, most of the things people have claimed are externalities in the
 literature are nothing of the sort.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We then get onto pecuniary externalities, where the definition is pretty
 similar.
 ``External economies are invoked whenever the profits of one producer are
 affected by the actions of other producers.'' (Scitovsky p.146)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
