\begin_layout Standard
The key quote: ``This definition of external economies obviously includes
 direct or nonmarket interdependence among producers, as discussed above
 and defined by Meade.
 It is much broader, however, than his definition, because, in addition
 to direct interdependence among producers, it also includes interdependence
 among producers through the market mechanism.
 This latter type of interdependence may be called `pecuniary external economies
' to distinguish it from the technological external economies of direct
 interdependence.'' (ibid.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Scitovsky notes this looks like 'interdependence through the market mechanism'
 and wonders why theorists at the time are claiming these externalities
 cause problems for equilibria, when an equilibrium ``
\emph on
relies
\emph default
 on market interdependence to bring about an optimum situation.'' 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
He explains pecuniary externalities brilliantly.
 So: a firm invests, and thus increases its efficiency.
 Its product thus costs less, and demand for inputs goes up - so they cost
 more.
 The PEs here: the firms who benefit from those higher prices.
 Also - though he doesn't mention it - of course, other firms relying on
 those inputs will see their costs 
\series bold
rise
\series default
.
 Later, here's Scitovsky pointing that out, p.149: ``Expansion in industry
 A may also give rise to profits (i) in an industry that produces a factor
 used in industry A, (ii) in an industry whose product is complementary
 in use to the product of industry A, (iii) in an industry whose product
 is a substitute for a factor used in industry A, or (iv) in an industry
 whose product is consumed by persons whose incomes are raised by the expansion
 of industry A-and this list does not include the cases in which the expansion
 causes external 
\emph on
dis
\emph default
economies.''
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There's also consumers' surplus - but I don't think that's an externality.
 (p.147.) This is Krugman's point, of course, though he doesn't refer back
 to Scitovsky: ``In competitive general equilibrium, of course, pecuniary
 externalities have no welfare significance and could not lead to the kind
 of interesting dynamics we shall derive later.
 Over the past decade, however, it has become a familiar point that in the
 presence of imperfect competition and increasing returns, pecuniary externaliti
es matter; for example, if one firm's actions affect the demand for the
 product of another firm whose price exceeds marginal cost, this is as much
 a 'real' externality as if one firm's research and development spills over
 into the general knowledge pool.)'' (increts p.485)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It would appear to be that the focus is on how these kinds of externalities
 can be understood, given that they appear to imply problems with general
 equilibrium.
 Note Krugman's argument above: they can't have any welfare significance.
 I don't know why not.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Anyway, the reasons he is giving why they won't fit in - sounding familiar.
 So, there's indivisibilities - which sound a lot like they could stem,
 here, from increasing returns.
 A firm can't match marginal cost to price; that would certainly be the
 case if they can't control the scale of their production under increasing
 returns.
 So he points out ``in such a case one of the available alternatives will
 still yield him a higher profit than all others; but this need no longer
 be the one that is also the best from society's point of view.'' (p.147)
 So, I think, e.g.
 that would be monopolistic - a firm is able to get a markup price, using
 monopoly power.
 How one can tell that's not best from society's point of view, I don't
 know.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note also that for Scitovsky, ``canals, roads, bridges, and railways - the
 typical examples of indivisibilities in economics.'' (p.148) Though indivisibilit
ies appears to mean something that 
