\end_inset

has a minimum size below which it is unavailable.'' (Google it.) Thus tied
 to economies of scale, in the form of fixed costs that must be met.
 Oh ahah.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
``Profits are a sign of disequilibrium; and the magnitude of profits, under
 free competition, may be regarded as a rough index of the degree of disequilibr
ium.'' (p.148) Note: core model - equilibrium has to come about through the
 entry and exit of firms driving profits to zero.
 Or as Scitovsky puts it: ``Profits in a freely competitive industry lead
 to investment in that industry; and the investment, in turn, tends to eliminate
 the profits that have called it forth.
 This far, then, investment tends to bring equilibrium nearer.'' That'd be
 one of those stylised facts, wouldn't it!?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
He notes, though, that equilibrium might not be the outcome - as with the
 externalities just discussed, making my products cheaper might input into
 another firm, and thus make them more profitable - leading once again to
 a disequilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Oh god - he then seems to get into an argument that price signals are simply
 not able to do the job of co-ordinating all the pecuniary externalities
 that arise in an economy.
 Well! Worth remembering: this is all argued in the context of general equilibri
um theory's welfare theorems - which of course I don't presently understand.
\end_layout

